  "question": {
    "text": "In young&#39;s double slit experiment performed using a monochromatic light of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math>, when a glass plate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>&#956;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> of thickness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mi>&#955;</mi></math> is introduced in the path of the one of the interfering beams, the intensity at the position where the central maximum occurred previously remains unchanged. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> will be",
    "image": null
  },
